<section class="band" id="pushback" aria-labelledby="pb-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Where I'd push back</span>
      <h2 id="pb-h">Six calls to make before anyone writes CSS</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="critique">
      <article class="crit"><span class="tag">Scope</span><h3>The app list is a month of work for one person</h3><p>Accounts, accessibility settings, private and public maps, a searchable community, and live alerts, plus the 3D viewer and routing you already own. The judged demo needs five screens. Everything else becomes a believable shell or gets cut. The build order below sorts it.</p></article>
      <article class="crit"><span class="tag">Accessibility</span><h3>Coral fails as text</h3><p>Coral on cream is 2.18:1 and white on coral is 2.6:1. Both fail WCAG. For a wayfinding product that talks about elevators and accessibility, a judge who checks contrast will notice. Use coral for fills, the route line and buttons with dark text (6.3:1). Use rust for coral-colored text.</p></article>
      <article class="crit"><span class="tag">Distinctiveness</span><h3>Cream, coral and a soft serif is the default AI look</h3><p>That combination is all over generated landing pages right now. Keep your colors but skip the serif. Lean on road-sign type, the dark forest green, and the scan itself, so the page looks like a wayfinding tool and not a template.</p></article>
      <article class="crit"><span class="tag">3D</span><h3>Point clouds wash out on cream</h3><p>Colored points need a dark field to read. Put every 3D view on near-black forest green and let the cream carry the text sections. The page alternates paper and night bands, the same way this page does.</p></article>
      <article class="crit"><span class="tag">Footprints</span><h3>Great motif, but not everyone walks</h3><p>Footprints sell "discover the unknown" on the landing page. Inside the app, use a route line with waypoints, because some of your users roll, and the elevator-outage feature is aimed at them. If someone asks about it in Q&amp;A, you'll already have an answer.</p></article>
      <article class="crit"><span class="tag">Booth</span><h3>The laptop should hand the demo to the judge</h3><p>Put a QR code in the hero and the Join section that opens the guest link on the judge's own phone. Add an idle loop that replays the hero every 20 seconds so the laptop looks alive between judges.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

